#!/bin/sh
# The moonraker service, as s6 runs it. Exec'd by s6-supervise, never by hand.
#
# WHAT A `run` SCRIPT IS. s6-svscan sees this directory in the scandir and
# starts one s6-supervise for it; that s6-supervise forks, puts the child in a
# process group of its own, and execs this file. The process this script turns
# into IS the service: when it exits, s6-supervise starts the whole thing
# again. Everything below follows from that one sentence.
#
# THEREFORE: NO start-stop-daemon, and no `-b`. init.d/S62moonraker used to
# start moonraker with `start-stop-daemon -S -b -m -p /run/moonraker.pid`,
# which forks the server into the background and returns. Under a supervisor
# that is exactly wrong: s6 would supervise the process that forked and exited,
# decide the service had died a millisecond after starting it, and start it
# again for ever, while the real moonraker it left behind went on serving
# :7125 supervised by nobody. So the invocation at the bottom is the same
# interpreter, the same entry point and the same `-d` argument as the old
# daemon line -- argument for argument -- with the backgrounding removed and
# `exec` in front.
#
# AND `exec`. Without it this shell would stay around as moonraker's parent, s6
# would be supervising the shell, and every signal it sent -- the SIGTERM
# behind `S62moonraker stop` above all -- would go to a shell that ignores it
# while moonraker carried on. exec makes the pid s6 holds the pid of the python
# process itself, which is also what makes `s6-svstat -p` the honest answer to
# "which moonraker is this printer running?" that the pidfile used to give.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT LOOK AT MOD_WEB. It cannot: it runs whenever s6 decides
# the service should be up, and a `run` that exits because it disagrees is a
# respawn loop with extra steps. The gate is the `down` file next to this one
# plus init.d/S62moonraker, which is the thing that reads anvil.conf at the
# point in the boot where reading it means something. See the comment inside
# `down`; that is where the whole MOD_WEB story is written.
#
# NOTHING HERE MOVES. The interpreter, the entry point, the data directory and
# the config moonraker reads out of it are all exactly where init.d/S62moonraker
# had them. Moving /usr/data/config is phase 7 of docs/notes/80-s6-migration.md
# and it is deliberately not this change: FlashForge's own klipperDaemon
# hardcodes /usr/data/config/printer.cfg, so the directory cannot move until we
# own the klippy launch as well. When it does move, the `-d` below is the one
# line in this file that changes.
MODDIR=/usr/data/anvil

# anvil.conf and anvil-env.sh are read HERE, not inherited, for the reason the
# camera's run script gives at the same point: s6-supervise starts this script
# from the SCANNER's environment, which is whatever firmwareExe had at boot --
# and on an `s6-svc -u` typed over ssh it is whatever that ssh session had,
# which is not the same thing at all. anvil-env.sh is the file that defines
# FF_PYTHON and LD_LIBRARY_PATH, and moonraker is the process that dies with
# "libpython3.8.so.1.0: cannot open shared object file" when that is wrong --
# so a supervised respawn that inherited a stranger's environment would be a
# service that works on the boot path and fails on every restart afterwards.
[ -f $MODDIR/anvil.conf ] && . $MODDIR/anvil.conf
[ -f $MODDIR/anvil-env.sh ] && . $MODDIR/anvil-env.sh

# ONE moonraker, at one path, and that path is on the DATA partition. The long
# version is in init.d/S62moonraker and it has not changed: the byte-identical
# second copy under /usr/prog is gone, because /usr/prog is the partition a
# stock FlashForge flash overwrites, and running from there made "which
# moonraker is this printer running?" depend on what was flashed last.
MOONRAKER_MAIN=$MODDIR/moonraker/moonraker.py
MOONRAKER_DATA=/usr/data
MOONRAKER_TMP=/usr/data/tmp
MOONRAKER_CONF=$MOONRAKER_DATA/config/moonraker.conf

# TMPDIR, which is one of the exactly two things FlashForge's moonrakerDaemon
# did for us that were worth keeping -- the other was the pidfile, and s6 has
# made that one redundant. Moonraker writes temporary files, uploads among
# them, and /tmp on this box is a ramdisk, so a large upload without this fills
# memory instead of the data partition. It is exported here rather than left to
# the init script because s6 restarts this service without going anywhere near
# init.d/S62moonraker: a service that only has its environment when a shell
# script happened to run first is not supervised, it is lucky.
#
# The wipe is unconditional here where the init script had to guard it on
# "moonraker is not already running". That guard existed because re-running
# S62moonraker over ssh against a LIVE server would otherwise delete the
# directory it was streaming an upload into. Under s6 it is satisfied by
# construction: s6-supervise runs exactly one copy of this service at a time
# and only execs this script after the previous one has been reaped, so at the
# moment this line runs there is no moonraker of ours anywhere.
rm -rf $MOONRAKER_TMP
mkdir -p $MOONRAKER_TMP $MOONRAKER_DATA/logs
export TMPDIR=$MOONRAKER_TMP

# ---- the leftover from an unsupervised start -------------------------------
#
# WHY THIS IS HERE. init.d/S62moonraker still has a fallback that starts
# moonraker directly when there is no scanner -- see the long argument in that
# file -- so a printer can reach the moment when the supervised service comes
# up with a hand-started moonraker already holding :7125. It can also reach it
# by way of an upgrade: a printer running the pre-phase-5 payload has a
# start-stop-daemon'd moonraker alive with its pid in /run/moonraker.pid, and
# installing this payload and typing `S62moonraker restart` rather than
# rebooting would leave it exactly there.
#
# What follows is not a graceful loss. Moonraker exits when it cannot bind its
# port, s6 starts it again a second later, it cannot bind again, and the loop
# runs for as long as the printer is on -- with `s6-svstat` saying "up" most of
# the time, because the process it is watching really is alive, it is just
# dying -- while what is actually answering Mainsail is an unsupervised
# leftover that nothing will ever restart. That is the same failure the nginx
# run script sweeps for, measured there first, and it is worth the same handful
# of lines here.
#
# It reads /proc rather than `ps | grep`, for the reason written out in the
# nginx run script: busybox `ps` truncates its output to the terminal width, so
# a long python command line arrives cut in half and a grep for the entry point
# matches nothing at all. /proc/PID/cmdline is the whole command line, always.
#
# The match is POSITIVE and narrow -- the absolute path of the entry point we
# are about to run, which appears in the command line of a moonraker and of
# nothing else on this printer. `s6-supervise moonraker`, this script and
# `init.d/S62moonraker start` all have the word moonraker in their command
# lines and none of them has moonraker.py's absolute path in it; our own pid is
# skipped as well, for the same belt-and-braces reason nginx skips it.
for _mr_proc in /proc/[0-9]*; do
    [ -r "$_mr_proc/cmdline" ] || continue
    [ "${_mr_proc#/proc/}" = "$$" ] && continue
    _mr_args=`tr '\0' ' ' < "$_mr_proc/cmdline" 2>/dev/null`
    case "$_mr_args" in
        *"$MOONRAKER_MAIN"*) kill -9 "${_mr_proc#/proc/}" 2>/dev/null ;;
    esac
done
unset _mr_proc _mr_args

# ---- readiness: fd 3, and what "ready" is allowed to mean ------------------
#
# The `notification-fd` file next to this script holds "3": this service is
# READY once a line is written to file descriptor 3, and `s6-svwait -U` blocks
# until it is. This is the capability runit has no concept of and the reason
# the supervisor choice went the way it did -- see tools/supervisor/README.md.
#
# WHY MOONRAKER GETS ONE AT ALL, when nginx does not. Because something already
# waits for it, badly. bin/ff-startup.py -- the program S70klipper hands the
# boot to -- polls http://127.0.0.1:7125/server/info in a retry loop to learn
# klippy_state, and every one of those early polls is against a moonraker that
# has been forked and is not listening yet. init.d/S62moonraker had its own
# version of the same guess: a detached moonraker_check that slept two seconds
# five times and called the service "up" if the pid was still there. A pid that
# is still there is not an API that answers, and the gap between the two is
# exactly the window in which moonraker imports its components and dies
# quietly.
#
# WHEN IS MOONRAKER READY? The candidates, and why only one of them is worth
# telling a caller:
#
#   "the process was forked"  -- worthless, and it is what the pidfile and the
#       old moonraker_check both actually measured. Component-load failures
#       happen seconds AFTER the fork, which is why that check had to sleep at
#       all, and a moonraker that is still importing components answers
#       nothing.
#   "klippy_state is ready"   -- WRONG, and tempting, because that is what
#       ff-startup.py is ultimately waiting for. But it is not a fact about
#       THIS service. Moonraker is up and doing its job on a printer whose MCU
#       never answered -- reporting klippy_state=error IS the job -- so a
#       readiness that waited for klippy would never fire on precisely the
#       broken printer whose UI somebody needs, and the caller would time out
#       and print a warning about a moonraker that was working perfectly. It
#       would also invert the dependency: moonraker would be declaring itself
#       ready on behalf of a service it neither owns nor starts.
#   "the HTTP API is listening" -- this one. Every consumer of this service --
#       Mainsail through nginx's /webcam-and-everything-else proxy,
#       HelixScreen, and ff-startup.py -- is an HTTP client, and what an HTTP
#       client needs is a socket that accepts. Moonraker binds its port after
#       the server object is built and its core components have imported, so a
#       bound port is also the strongest available statement about the
#       component failures the old check was written to catch.
#
# HOW IT IS CHECKED, and why it needs no tool. /proc/net/tcp lists every TCP
# socket on the box: field 2 is LOCAL_ADDRESS:PORT with the port in uppercase
# hex, field 4 is the state, and 0A is TCP_LISTEN. No nc -- this busybox has no
# nc applet at all, measured on the replica -- and no wget in the hot path of a
# supervised service. The address half is deliberately not matched: moonraker
# binds 0.0.0.0 under the shipped config, and a user who sets host: 127.0.0.1
# should still count as listening.
#
# THE PORT IS READ FROM MOONRAKER'S OWN CONFIG, not written down here. 7125 is
# the shipped default and also moonraker's, but it is a user-editable line in a
# user-editable file, and a probe watching the wrong port would report a
# perfectly healthy moonraker as never ready -- which is worse than having no
# probe at all, because S62moonraker would then print a warning and a log tail
# on every boot of a printer that is working. The awk is section-aware so that
# a `port:` belonging to some other component cannot be picked up by mistake.
MOONRAKER_PORT=`awk '
    /^[[:space:]]*\[/ { section = $0 }
    section ~ /\[[[:space:]]*server[[:space:]]*\]/ && /^[[:space:]]*port[[:space:]]*:/ {
        sub(/^[^:]*:[[:space:]]*/, "");
        gsub(/[^0-9]/, "");
        if (length($0)) { print; exit }
    }' "$MOONRAKER_CONF" 2>/dev/null`
[ -n "$MOONRAKER_PORT" ] || MOONRAKER_PORT=7125

# The prober is a subshell because the last thing this script does is exec, and
# an exec'd moonraker cannot report on itself. It inherits fd 3 across the
# fork; the exec below closes fd 3 in MOONRAKER (3>&-) so that the only process
# holding the notification pipe is the one with something to say about it --
# were moonraker to keep it open, s6 would never see the pipe close when the
# prober gave up.
#
# 180 seconds, which is much longer than the camera's 60 and is not a copied
# number. Moonraker imports its whole component tree on a 1GHz mipsel box off
# eMMC; on the qemu replica the same startup is slower again, and
# case-moonraker.sh has waited up to 105 seconds for the API since long before
# s6 was involved. A prober that gave up first would turn a slow printer into a
# printer that reports itself broken. Giving up simply means no notification is
# ever sent, which is the honest outcome: S62moonraker then prints its warning
# and the tail of moonraker.log, exactly as its old detached check did.
(
    _mr_n=0
    while [ $_mr_n -lt 180 ]; do
        if awk -v p=":`printf '%04X' "$MOONRAKER_PORT"`" \
               '$2 ~ p"$" && $4 == "0A" { found = 1 }
                END { exit !found }' /proc/net/tcp 2>/dev/null
        then
            echo ready >&3
            exit 0
        fi
        sleep 1
        _mr_n=$((_mr_n + 1))
    done
) &

# THE INVOCATION, preserved argument for argument from the start-stop-daemon
# line it replaces:
#
#     start-stop-daemon -S -b -m -p /run/moonraker.pid --exec "$FF_PYTHON" \
#         -- "$MODDIR/moonraker/moonraker.py" -d /usr/data
#
# `-d /usr/data` is moonraker's data path: it is where it finds
# config/moonraker.conf, where it writes logs/moonraker.log and where its
# database lives. It is not a debug flag and it is not optional -- moonraker
# defaults to ~/printer_data, which on this printer is a home directory that
# does not exist.
#
# stderr is inherited from s6-supervise, which inherited it from the scanner,
# which init.d/S40s6 points at /usr/data/logs/s6.log. That is a genuine
# improvement rather than an accident of the port: under start-stop-daemon -b
# these descriptors went to /dev/null, so a moonraker that died before its own
# logging was configured -- a syntax error in a component, a missing shared
# object -- died with its explanation thrown away, and the only symptom was a
# service that "did not come up". Now it lands in a file that outlives the
# boot. Moonraker's own structured log stays exactly where it has always been,
# at $MOONRAKER_DATA/logs/moonraker.log, because moonraker opens that itself
# from -d.
#
# nice -n "${NICE_MOONRAKER:-0}", not a plain exec of the interpreter: this
# service is supervised now, which init.d/S62moonraker's start_unsupervised()
# fallback is not, and NICE_MOONRAKER (anvil.conf) exists to protect klippy
# from exactly the process this script starts -- reaching only the fallback
# would leave the feature dead for every printer that has s6, which by
# default is all of them. `nice` sets its own priority and then execs the
# given command in its own place (verified: the pid s6 ends up holding is the
# interpreter's, not nice's, exactly as required above), so this is still one
# process and $FF_PYTHON is still what --exec would have matched had this
# been start-stop-daemon. 0 or unset = normal priority, unchanged from before
# this existed.
exec nice -n "${NICE_MOONRAKER:-0}" "$FF_PYTHON" "$MOONRAKER_MAIN" -d "$MOONRAKER_DATA" 3>&-
